HTTP(HyperText Transfer Protocol)
QUIC - is a modern transport protocol developed by Google and standardized by the IETF
       in 2021. It's the foundation of HTTP/3.

HTTP Methods:

Method  | Purpose                      | Has Body?
##################################################
GET     | Retrieve a resource          | No
POST    | Submit data, create resource | Yes
PUT     | Replace a resource entirely  | Yes
PATCH   | Partially update a resource  | Yes
DELETE  | Remove a resource            | No
HEAD    | Like GET, but headers only   | No
OPTIONS | Ask what methods are allowed | No

Key distinctions:

Get -> read only, should never change data
POST -> creates something new, not idempotent
PUT -> full replacement, idempotent
PATCH -> partial update
DELETE -> remove, idempotent

idempotent = doing it multiple times has the same effect as once.

Status codes:

1xx - Infomational
100 Continue -> Keep sending your request
101 Switching Protocols Upgrading

2xx - Success
200 OK -> Standart success
201 Created -> Resource was created(after POST)
204 No Content -> Success but nothing to return

3xx - Redirection
301 Moved Permanently -> Resource has a new URL, update your links
302 Found -> Temporary redirect
304 Not Modified -> Use your cached version

4xx - Client Error
400 Bad Request -> Malformed request
401 Unauthorized -> Need to authenticate
403 Forbidden -> Authenticated but not allowed
404 Not Found -> Resource doesn't exist
405 Method Not Allowed -> Wrong HTTP method
409 Conflict -> Conflict with current state
422 Unprocessable -> Validation failed
429 Too Many Request -> Rate limited

5xx - Server Error
500 Internal Server Error -> Generic server crash
502 Bad Gateaway -> Upstream server failed
503 Service Unavailable -> Server overloaded or down
504 Gateaway Timeout -> Upstream server too slow

Headers:

Common request:

Host -> Which domain you're contacting
Authorization -> Auth token/credentials
Content-Type -> Format of the body you're sending
Accept -> Format you want back
Cookie -> Session data
User-Agent -> Browser/Client identifier

Common response:

Content-Type -> Format of the body being returned
Set-Cookie -> Tell browser to store a cookie
Cache-Control -> How long to cache the response
Location -> Where to redirect(used with 3xx)
CORS headers -> Cross-origin permissions

HTTP versions:

HTTP/1.1 - One request at a time per connection, keep-alive
HTTP/2   - Multiplexing - many requests over one connection, compressed headers
HTTP/3   - Runs on UDP(via QUIC) instead of TCP, lower latency

The mental model:

Method -> What you want to do
URL -> What you want to do it to
Headers -> Metadata about the request
Body -> Data you're sending(POST, PUT, PATCH)
Status -> Did it work?
Headers -> Metadata about the response
Body -> Data comming back
